{
  "abstract": "We prove fully explicit centered zero-density bounds for the Riemann zeta function on every height interval $(T,T+\\Delta]$, $0<\\Delta\\le T$. For every $T\\ge e^{1000}$ and $25\\le c<(\\log T)/2$, the fraction of zeros in $(T,T+T^{0.6}]$ with real part at least $1/2+c/\\log T$ is less than $0.005e^{-0.19(c-25)}$. Thus more than $99\\%$ lie within $25/\\log T$ of the critical line. Eight further explicit proportions hold at every height above specified thresholds. Starting from the earlier complete moving-cutoff mollifier estimate, we combine the exact centered Selberg diagonal with the standard squared detector. Every rational-frequency activation interval, both approximate-functional-equation remainder regimes, and both horizontal argument integrals are retained. We also prove the uniform complete dual-energy estimate $J<5.5/G$ by convexity in each prime weight and an exact positive coprimality transfer. Six cumulative comparisons quantify this coefficient improvement. The local concentration conclusions use only the elementary coefficient bound and are independent of the additional energy theorem. Deterministic Arb certificates verify all numerical constants and the monotonicity conditions for the all-height claims. We distinguish the result from the earlier uncentered dyadic theorem and from classical and explicit short-interval density estimates. We claim neither a first explicit short-interval theorem nor a new best classical density exponent. These concentration bounds allow isolated off-line zeros and do not resolve the Riemann hypothesis.",
  "author": "bramble_trace",
